Ember's organisation policy: notes for policy.example.json
==========================================================

Copy policy.example.json to /etc/ember/policy.json and change what you need. The file must belong to root and be
writable only by root. Ember reads it again every 10 seconds: when it changes, Ember stops everything and asks to be
restarted, and the restart reads the new policy whole. The setup check (ember doctor) shows whether it is in force.

A policy can only make Ember stricter than having no policy at all. If the file can't be read, isn't valid JSON or has
a key or value Ember doesn't know, Ember locks down (no actions, no listening) until it is fixed. JSON has no comments:
a "comment" key is a mistake too, so keep notes outside the file. Ember 0.1.0 doesn't know the keys added in 0.1.1
("features.aiReview", "features.memory", "features.routines", "features.updates", "features.extensions",
"features.autonomy", "features.userProvider" and "approvals"): it reads them as mistakes and locks down, so update
Ember on every computer before deploying a policy that uses them. Builds from before 0.1.0 do the same with "commands", "ssh",
"features.wsl" and "features.ssh".

Important: a policy without "commands" turns commands off on this computer, in WSL and over SSH, because the
administrator never agreed to them. The example turns them on for each kind of computer; set any of them to "ask"
(every command asks first) or "off".

Every key is optional:

  "features"            true or false for each: "voice" (listening for "Hey Ember"), "speak" (replies out loud),
                        "actions" (acting in apps at all; false also turns commands off), "cursor" (borrowing the
                        mouse and keyboard), "desk" (Ember's private desk), "screenImages" (screenshots sent to the
                        model), "wsl" (WSL apps and the WSL computer), "ssh" (connecting to other computers),
                        "aiReview" (false: Assisted's check is off: no step or command is sent to a model to be
                        checked against the user's words, what it would have checked asks, and Assisted can't be
                        chosen), "memory" (false: Ember remembers nothing between runs and learns nothing on its
                        own), "routines" (false: no routines, and reminders end when Ember quits), "updates" (false:
                        Ember never asks GitHub whether a new version is out), "extensions" (false: no extension
                        loads, runs or can be added), "autonomy" (false: no approval mode is in force, so Ember
                        asks before ordinary steps) and "userProvider" (false: users can't send their requests and
                        screenshots to a model service of their own; the organisation's "provider" turns it off too)
  "neverLook"           apps and windows Ember never reads, captures or acts in (added to each user's list)
  "confirmEveryAction"  true: a click before every action, every time
  "allowGrants"         false: no "Always in this app"
  "maxActionsPerMinute" 1 to 40, lower than Ember's own limit
  "models"              "allowed" (only these model ids), "conductor" and "worker" (pin the models used)
  "provider"            the organisation's own model endpoint instead of GitHub sign-in: "type" ("openai", "azure"
                        or "anthropic"), "baseUrl", "apiKeyFile" (a file only Ember's users can read), "wireApi"
                        ("completions" or "responses": the API an "openai" endpoint speaks) and, for Azure,
                        "azure": { "apiVersion": "…" } (the API version; nowhere else)
  "auditRetentionDays"  how long the audit log is kept (default 30)
  "approvals"           "maxMode": the highest approval mode users may choose ("interactive", "assisted" or
                        "autopilot"); a higher choice works as this one at most, and it turns nothing on
  "commands"            "local", "wsl" and "ssh": "on" (allowed; each user still turns them on), "ask" or "off"
  "ssh"                 "allowedHosts" (patterns of the only computers Ember may connect to: * matches any run of
                        characters, ? one character; ["*"] allows every computer, [] none), "desktop" (false:
                        commands only on remotes), "requireConsoleConsent" (on a remote: a person at its screen must
                        allow Ember first), "runtimeMirror" (an https:// mirror of nodejs.org/dist for remotes) and
                        "commandGraceSeconds" (3 to 120)

The example leaves "allowedHosts" out, so Ember may connect to every computer. To limit SSH to some, add a list:

  "ssh": { "allowedHosts": ["build-*.example.com", "deploy@db-01.example.com:22"], "desktop": true }

Ember's developer guide (docs/DEVELOPING.md, "For administrators") and docs/COMMANDS-WSL-SSH.md section 6 explain
each key in full, including how the policy of a computer Ember connects to applies there.
